_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[{"regions":[],"skip_binder":[{"Deduplicated":31698},{"Var":{"Free":0}}]}],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":24,"beg":{"line":936,"col":4},"end":{"line":936,"col":43}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"name":"array_mut","ty":{"HashConsedValue":[31828,{"Ref":[{"Var":{"Free":0}},{"HashConsedValue":[31827,{"Slice":{"Deduplicated":31700}}]},"Mut"]}]}},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":24,"beg":{"line":938,"col":4},"end":{"line":938,"col":26}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"name":"initialized","ty":{"Deduplicated":1670}}]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":24,"align":8,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[0,16],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},null,{"def_id":101,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["pin",0]},{"Ident":["Pin",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":85,"beg":{"line":1092,"col":0},"end":{"line":1092,"col":19}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" A pointer which pins its pointee in place."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`Pin`] is a wrapper around some kind of pointer `Ptr` which makes that pointer \"pin\" its"},{"DocComment":" pointee value in place, thus preventing the value referenced by that pointer from being moved"},{"DocComment":" or otherwise invalidated at that place in memory unless it implements [`Unpin`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" *See the [`pin` module] documentation for a more thorough exploration of pinning.*"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Pinning values with [`Pin<Ptr>`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In order to pin a value, we wrap a *pointer to that value* (of some type `Ptr`) in a"},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`]. [`Pin<Ptr>`] can wrap any pointer type, forming a promise that the **pointee**"},{"DocComment":" will not be *moved* or [otherwise invalidated][subtle-details]. If the pointee value's type"},{"DocComment":" implements [`Unpin`], we are free to disregard these requirements entirely and can wrap any"},{"DocComment":" pointer to that value in [`Pin`] directly via [`Pin::new`]. If the pointee value's type does"},{"DocComment":" not implement [`Unpin`], then Rust will not let us use the [`Pin::new`] function directly and"},{"DocComment":" we'll need to construct a [`Pin`]-wrapped pointer in one of the more specialized manners"},{"DocComment":" discussed below."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" We call such a [`Pin`]-wrapped pointer a **pinning pointer** (or pinning ref, or pinning"},{"DocComment":" [`Box`], etc.) because its existence is the thing that is pinning the underlying pointee in"},{"DocComment":" place: it is the metaphorical \"pin\" securing the data in place on the pinboard (in memory)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It is important to stress that the thing in the [`Pin`] is not the value which we want to pin"},{"DocComment":" itself, but rather a pointer to that value! A [`Pin<Ptr>`] does not pin the `Ptr` but rather"},{"DocComment":" the pointer's ***pointee** value*."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The most common set of types which require pinning related guarantees for soundness are the"},{"DocComment":" compiler-generated state machines that implement [`Future`] for the return value of"},{"DocComment":" `async fn`s. These compiler-generated [`Future`]s may contain self-referential pointers, one"},{"DocComment":" of the most common use cases for [`Pin`]. More details on this point are provided in the"},{"DocComment":" [`pin` module] docs, but suffice it to say they require the guarantees provided by pinning to"},{"DocComment":" be implemented soundly."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This requirement for the implementation of `async fn`s means that the [`Future`] trait"},{"DocComment":" requires all calls to [`poll`] to use a <code>self: [Pin]\\<&mut Self></code> parameter instead"},{"DocComment":" of the usual `&mut self`. Therefore, when manually polling a future, you will need to pin it"},{"DocComment":" first."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" You may notice that `async fn`-sourced [`Future`]s are only a small percentage of all"},{"DocComment":" [`Future`]s that exist, yet we had to modify the signature of [`poll`] for all [`Future`]s"},{"DocComment":" to accommodate them. This is unfortunate, but there is a way that the language attempts to"},{"DocComment":" alleviate the extra friction that this API choice incurs: the [`Unpin`] trait."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The vast majority of Rust types have no reason to ever care about being pinned. These"},{"DocComment":" types implement the [`Unpin`] trait, which entirely opts all values of that type out of"},{"DocComment":" pinning-related guarantees. For values of these types, pinning a value by pointing to it with a"},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`] will have no actual effect."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The reason this distinction exists is exactly to allow APIs like [`Future::poll`] to take a"},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`] as an argument for all types while only forcing [`Future`] types that actually"},{"DocComment":" care about pinning guarantees pay the ergonomics cost. For the majority of [`Future`] types"},{"DocComment":" that don't have a reason to care about being pinned and therefore implement [`Unpin`], the"},{"DocComment":" <code>[Pin]\\<&mut Self></code> will act exactly like a regular `&mut Self`, allowing direct"},{"DocComment":" access to the underlying value. Only types that *don't* implement [`Unpin`] will be restricted."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ### Pinning a value of a type that implements [`Unpin`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If the type of the value you need to \"pin\" implements [`Unpin`], you can trivially wrap any"},{"DocComment":" pointer to that value in a [`Pin`] by calling [`Pin::new`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::pin::Pin;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Create a value of a type that implements `Unpin`"},{"DocComment":" let mut unpin_future = std::future::ready(5);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Pin it by creating a pinning mutable reference to it (ready to be `poll`ed!)"},{"DocComment":" let my_pinned_unpin_future: Pin<&mut _> = Pin::new(&mut unpin_future);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ### Pinning a value inside a [`Box`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The simplest and most flexible way to pin a value that does not implement [`Unpin`] is to put"},{"DocComment":" that value inside a [`Box`] and then turn that [`Box`] into a \"pinning [`Box`]\" by wrapping it"},{"DocComment":" in a [`Pin`]. You can do both of these in a single step using [`Box::pin`]. Let's see an"},{"DocComment":" example of using this flow to pin a [`Future`] returned from calling an `async fn`, a common"},{"DocComment":" use case as described above."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::pin::Pin;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" async fn add_one(x: u32) -> u32 {"},{"DocComment":"     x + 1"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Call the async function to get a future back"},{"DocComment":" let fut = add_one(42);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Pin the future inside a pinning box"},{"DocComment":" let pinned_fut: Pin<Box<_>> = Box::pin(fut);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you have a value which is already boxed, for example a [`Box<dyn Future>`][Box], you can pin"},{"DocComment":" that value in-place at its current memory address using [`Box::into_pin`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::pin::Pin;"},{"DocComment":" use std::future::Future;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" async fn add_one(x: u32) -> u32 {"},{"DocComment":"     x + 1"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" fn boxed_add_one(x: u32) -> Box<dyn Future<Output = u32>> {"},{"DocComment":"     Box::new(add_one(x))"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let boxed_fut = boxed_add_one(42);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Pin the future inside the existing box"},{"DocComment":" let pinned_fut: Pin<Box<_>> = Box::into_pin(boxed_fut);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" There are similar pinning methods offered on the other standard library smart pointer types"},{"DocComment":" as well, like [`Rc`] and [`Arc`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ### Pinning a value on the stack using [`pin!`]"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" There are some situations where it is desirable or even required (for example, in a `#[no_std]`"},{"DocComment":" context where you don't have access to the standard library or allocation in general) to"},{"DocComment":" pin a value which does not implement [`Unpin`] to its location on the stack. Doing so is"},{"DocComment":" possible using the [`pin!`] macro. See its documentation for more."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Layout and ABI"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`Pin<Ptr>`] is guaranteed to have the same memory layout and ABI[^noalias] as `Ptr`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [^noalias]: There is a bit of nuance here that is still being decided about whether the"},{"DocComment":" aliasing semantics of `Pin<&mut T>` should be different than `&mut T`, but this is true as of"},{"DocComment":" today."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`pin!`]: crate::pin::pin \"pin!\""},{"DocComment":" [`Future`]: crate::future::Future \"Future\""},{"DocComment":" [`poll`]: crate::future::Future::poll \"Future::poll\""},{"DocComment":" [`Future::poll`]: crate::future::Future::poll \"Future::poll\""},{"DocComment":" [`pin` module]: self \"pin module\""},{"DocComment":" [`Rc`]: ../../std/rc/struct.Rc.html \"Rc\""},{"DocComment":" [`Arc`]: ../../std/sync/struct.Arc.html \"Arc\""},{"DocComment":" [Box]: ../../std/boxed/struct.Box.html \"Box\""},{"DocComment":" [`Box`]: ../../std/boxed/struct.Box.html \"Box\""},{"DocComment":" [`Box::pin`]: ../../std/boxed/struct.Box.html#method.pin \"Box::pin\""},{"DocComment":" [`Box::into_pin`]: ../../std/boxed/struct.Box.html#method.into_pin \"Box::into_pin\""},{"DocComment":" [subtle-details]: self#subtle-details-and-the-drop-guarantee \"pin subtle details\""},{"DocComment":" [`unsafe`]: ../../std/keyword.unsafe.html \"keyword unsafe\""}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":"pin"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"Ptr"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":85,"beg":{"line":1093,"col":4},"end":{"line":1093,"col":16}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"pointer","ty":{"Deduplicated":31698}}]},"layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},null,{"def_id":103,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["mem",0]},{"Ident":["alignment",0]},{"Ident":["Alignment",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":86,"beg":{"line":17,"col":0},"end":{"line":17,"col":20}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" A type storing a `usize` which is a power of two, and thus"},{"DocComment":" represents a possible alignment in the Rust abstract machine."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that particularly large alignments, while representable in this type,"},{"DocComment":" are likely not to be supported by actual allocators and linkers."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":86,"beg":{"line":21,"col":4},"end":{"line":21,"col":36}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"_inner_repr_trick","ty":{"HashConsedValue":[31829,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":115},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]}}]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":8,"align":8,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[0],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":true,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":104,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["alloc",0]},{"Ident":["layout",0]},{"Ident":["Layout",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":87,"beg":{"line":30,"col":0},"end":{"line":30,"col":17}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Layout of a block of memory."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" An instance of `Layout` describes a particular layout of memory."},{"DocComment":" You build a `Layout` up as an input to give to an allocator."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" All layouts have an associated size and a power-of-two alignment. The size, when rounded up to"},{"DocComment":" the nearest multiple of `align`, does not overflow `isize` (i.e., the rounded value will always be"},{"DocComment":" less than or equal to `isize::MAX`)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" (Note that layouts are *not* required to have non-zero size,"},{"DocComment":" even though `GlobalAlloc` requires that all memory requests"},{"DocComment":" be non-zero in size. A caller must either ensure that conditions"},{"DocComment":" like this are met, use specific allocators with looser"},{"DocComment":" requirements, or use the more lenient `Allocator` interface.)"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":"alloc_layout"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":87,"beg":{"line":32,"col":4},"end":{"line":32,"col":15}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"size","ty":{"Deduplicated":1670}},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":87,"beg":{"line":41,"col":4},"end":{"line":41,"col":20}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"align","ty":{"Deduplicated":14474}}]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":16,"align":8,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[8,0],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":105,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["fmt",0]},{"Ident":["Formatter",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":68,"beg":{"line":561,"col":0},"end":{"line":561,"col":24}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Configuration for formatting."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" A `Formatter` represents various options related to formatting. Users do not"},{"DocComment":" construct `Formatter`s directly; a mutable reference to one is passed to"},{"DocComment":" the `fmt` method of all formatting traits, like [`Debug`] and [`Display`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" To interact with a `Formatter`, you'll call various methods to change the"},{"DocComment":" various options related to formatting. For examples, please see the"},{"DocComment":" documentation of the methods defined on `Formatter` below."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":"Formatter"},"generics":{"regions":[{"index":0,"name":"'a","mutability":"Mutable"}],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[{"regions":[],"skip_binder":[{"Var":{"Free":0}},{"Var":{"Free":0}}]}],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":68,"beg":{"line":562,"col":4},"end":{"line":562,"col":30}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"options","ty":{"HashConsedValue":[16823,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":116},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]}},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":68,"beg":{"line":564,"col":4},"end":{"line":564,"col":33}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"buf","ty":{"HashConsedValue":[31831,{"Ref":[{"Var":{"Free":0}},{"HashConsedValue":[31830,{"DynTrait":{"binder":{"params":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"_dyn"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":19,"beg":{"line":1,"col":0},"end":{"line":1,"col":0}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"Dyn","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":40,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":2272}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[{"regions":[],"skip_binder":[{"Deduplicated":2272},{"Var":{"Free":0}}]}],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"skip_binder":{"Deduplicated":2273},"kind":"Dyn"}}}]},"Mut"]}]}}]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":24,"align":8,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[16,0],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":106,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["fmt",0]},{"Ident":["Error",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":68,"beg":{"line":109,"col":0},"end":{"line":109,"col":16}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" The error type which is returned from formatting a message into a stream."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This type does not support transmission of an error other than that an error"},{"DocComment":" occurred. This is because, despite the existence of this error,"},{"DocComment":" string formatting is considered an infallible operation."},{"DocComment":" `fmt()` implementors should not return this `Error` unless they received it from their"},{"DocComment":" [`Formatter`]. The only time your code should create a new instance of this"},{"DocComment":" error is when implementing `fmt::Write`, in order to cancel the formatting operation when"},{"DocComment":" writing to the underlying stream fails."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Any extra information must be arranged to be transmitted through some other means,"},{"DocComment":" such as storing it in a field to be consulted after the formatting operation has been"},{"DocComment":" cancelled. (For example, this is how [`std::io::Write::write_fmt()`] propagates IO errors"},{"DocComment":" during writing.)"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This type, `fmt::Error`, should not be"},{"DocComment":" confused with [`std::io::Error`] or [`std::error::Error`], which you may also"},{"DocComment":" have in scope."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`std::io::Error`]: ../../std/io/struct.Error.html"},{"DocComment":" [`std::io::Write::write_fmt()`]: ../../std/io/trait.Write.html#method.write_fmt"},{"DocComment":" [`std::error::Error`]: ../../std/error/trait.Error.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" use std::fmt::{self, write};"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut output = String::new();"},{"DocComment":" if let Err(fmt::Error) = write(&mut output, format_args!(\"Hello {}!\", \"world\")) {"},{"DocComment":"     panic!(\"An error occurred\");"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":0,"align":1,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":107,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["marker",0]},{"Ident":["PhantomData",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":7,"beg":{"line":811,"col":0},"end":{"line":811,"col":39}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Zero-sized type used to mark things that \"act like\" they own a `T`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Adding a `PhantomData<T>` field to your type tells the compiler that your"},{"DocComment":" type acts as though it stores a value of type `T`, even though it doesn't"},{"DocComment":" really. This information is used when computing certain safety properties."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For a more in-depth explanation of how to use `PhantomData<T>`, please see"},{"DocComment":" [the Nomicon](../../nomicon/phantom-data.html)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # A ghastly note 👻👻👻"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Though they both have scary names, `PhantomData` and 'phantom types' are"},{"DocComment":" related, but not identical. A phantom type parameter is simply a type"},{"DocComment":" parameter which is never used. In Rust, this often causes the compiler to"},{"DocComment":" complain, and the solution is to add a \"dummy\" use by way of `PhantomData`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused lifetime parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Perhaps the most common use case for `PhantomData` is a struct that has an"},{"DocComment":" unused lifetime parameter, typically as part of some unsafe code. For"},{"DocComment":" example, here is a struct `Slice` that has two pointers of type `*const T`,"},{"DocComment":" presumably pointing into an array somewhere:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```compile_fail,E0392"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The intention is that the underlying data is only valid for the"},{"DocComment":" lifetime `'a`, so `Slice` should not outlive `'a`. However, this"},{"DocComment":" intent is not expressed in the code, since there are no uses of"},{"DocComment":" the lifetime `'a` and hence it is not clear what data it applies"},{"DocComment":" to. We can correct this by telling the compiler to act *as if* the"},{"DocComment":" `Slice` struct contained a reference `&'a T`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # #[allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This also in turn infers the lifetime bound `T: 'a`, indicating"},{"DocComment":" that any references in `T` are valid over the lifetime `'a`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" When initializing a `Slice` you simply provide the value"},{"DocComment":" `PhantomData` for the field `phantom`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":" # struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":" #     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" fn borrow_vec<T>(vec: &Vec<T>) -> Slice<'_, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     let ptr = vec.as_ptr();"},{"DocComment":"     Slice {"},{"DocComment":"         start: ptr,"},{"DocComment":"         end: unsafe { ptr.add(vec.len()) },"},{"DocComment":"         phantom: PhantomData,"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused type parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It sometimes happens that you have unused type parameters which"},{"DocComment":" indicate what type of data a struct is \"tied\" to, even though that"},{"DocComment":" data is not actually found in the struct itself. Here is an"},{"DocComment":" example where this arises with [FFI]. The foreign interface uses"},{"DocComment":" handles of type `*mut ()` to refer to Rust values of different"},{"DocComment":" types. We track the Rust type using a phantom type parameter on"},{"DocComment":" the struct `ExternalResource` which wraps a handle."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [FFI]: ../../book/ch19-01-unsafe-rust.html#using-extern-functions-to-call-external-code"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # trait ResType { }"},{"DocComment":" # struct ParamType;"},{"DocComment":" # mod foreign_lib {"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn new(_: usize) -> *mut () { 42 as *mut () }"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn do_stuff(_: *mut (), _: usize) {}"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" # fn convert_params(_: ParamType) -> usize { 42 }"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct ExternalResource<R> {"},{"DocComment":"    resource_handle: *mut (),"},{"DocComment":"    resource_type: PhantomData<R>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl<R: ResType> ExternalResource<R> {"},{"DocComment":"     fn new() -> Self {"},{"DocComment":"         let size_of_res = size_of::<R>();"},{"DocComment":"         Self {"},{"DocComment":"             resource_handle: foreign_lib::new(size_of_res),"},{"DocComment":"             resource_type: PhantomData,"},{"DocComment":"         }"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":"     fn do_stuff(&self, param: ParamType) {"},{"DocComment":"         let foreign_params = convert_params(param);"},{"DocComment":"         foreign_lib::do_stuff(self.resource_handle, foreign_params);"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Ownership and the drop check"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The exact interaction of `PhantomData` with drop check **may change in the future**."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Currently, adding a field of type `PhantomData<T>` indicates that your type *owns* data of type"},{"DocComment":" `T` in very rare circumstances. This in turn has effects on the Rust compiler's [drop check]"},{"DocComment":" analysis. For the exact rules, see the [drop check] documentation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Layout"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For all `T`, the following are guaranteed:"},{"DocComment":" * `size_of::<PhantomData<T>>() == 0`"},{"DocComment":" * `align_of::<PhantomData<T>>() == 1`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [drop check]: Drop#drop-check"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":"phantom_data"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":0,"align":1,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false