 the argument's value is statically known at"},{"DocComment":" compile-time."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This is useful when there is a way of writing the code that will"},{"DocComment":" be *faster* when some variables have known values, but *slower*"},{"DocComment":" in the general case: an `if is_val_statically_known(var)` can be used"},{"DocComment":" to select between these two variants. The `if` will be optimized away"},{"DocComment":" and only the desired branch remains."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Formally speaking, this function non-deterministically returns `true`"},{"DocComment":" or `false`, and the caller has to ensure sound behavior for both cases."},{"DocComment":" In other words, the following code has *Undefined Behavior*:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```no_run"},{"DocComment":" #![feature(core_intrinsics)]"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(internal_features)]"},{"DocComment":" use std::hint::unreachable_unchecked;"},{"DocComment":" use std::intrinsics::is_val_statically_known;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" if !is_val_statically_known(0) { unsafe { unreachable_unchecked(); } }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This also means that the following code's behavior is unspecified; it"},{"DocComment":" may panic, or it may not:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```no_run"},{"DocComment":" #![feature(core_intrinsics)]"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(internal_features)]"},{"DocComment":" use std::intrinsics::is_val_statically_known;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(is_val_statically_known(0), is_val_statically_known(0));"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Unsafe code may not rely on `is_val_statically_known` returning any"},{"DocComment":" particular value, ever. However, the compiler will generally make it"},{"DocComment":" return `true` only if the value of the argument is actually known."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Type Requirements"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `T` must be either a `bool`, a `char`, a primitive numeric type (e.g. `f32`,"},{"DocComment":" but not `NonZeroISize`), or any thin pointer (e.g. `*mut String`)."},{"DocComment":" Any other argument types *may* cause a compiler error."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Pointers"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" When the input is a pointer, only the pointer itself is"},{"DocComment":" ever considered. The pointee has no effect. Currently, these functions"},{"DocComment":" behave identically:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" #![feature(core_intrinsics)]"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(internal_features)]"},{"DocComment":" use std::intrinsics::is_val_statically_known;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" fn foo(x: &i32) -> bool {"},{"DocComment":"     is_val_statically_known(x)"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" fn bar(x: &i32) -> bool {"},{"DocComment":"     is_val_statically_known("},{"DocComment":"         (x as *const i32).addr()"},{"DocComment":"     )"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" # _ = foo(&5_i32);"},{"DocComment":" # _ = bar(&5_i32);"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":71,"beg":{"line":2541,"col":40},"end":{"line":2541,"col":44}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnFn","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":16,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":31698}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}},{"clause_id":1,"span":{"data":{"file_id":19,"beg":{"line":1,"col":0},"end":{"line":1,"col":0}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"WhereClauseOnFn","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":0,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":31698}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints"